Survey Title Recommendations
The current title "LEAF Feedback Request" is generic and doesn't signal the topic or urgency. Consider something more specific and action-oriented like: "Share Your Input: LEAF Workflow Enhancements"
The title should do two things: identify the system (LEAF) and signal that this is about specific, actionable features users will recognize.

Introduction / Description Block
Add a quick 3-bullet overview so respondents know what to expect before diving in. Also identify that their responses are anonymous. Something like:
"This survey covers two topics. Here's what to expect:"

🔹 Paused Workflow Steps - How "On Hold" steps should behave in the system
🔹 Requestor Cancellation Permissions - Whether requestors should be able to cancel their own submissions
🔹 Stay Connected - Optional follow-up contact information

This respects respondents' time and sets clear expectations, a core HCD principle.

Section 1: Paused Workflow Steps

At the top of this section, explicitly call out the affected LEAF feature: "LEAF Core Feature: Workflow Editor" This helps users immediately anchor the context to something they know and use. Then tie in the section topic beneath it, for example: "Within the Workflow Editor, this section focuses on how paused or 'On Hold' steps behave throughout the system."
The "We Could Use Your Feedback" graphic block currently sits between the Future State content and the survey questions. Rather than using this as a visual break with a distorted graphic, this is a natural place to insert a transition sentence into the existing block. Replace or supplement the graphic callout with something like: "Now that you've reviewed the current and future state of Paused Workflow Steps, we'd love to hear how this applies to your team." This repurposes that block meaningfully rather than letting it function as decorative filler. Note: if you decide to keep some kind of graphic here, the graphic currently in this block is getting distorted and is a visual distraction. Please reach out for help adjusting this before sending.
For Question 4, recommend adding a scope clarifier before the response options: "Think about all workflows your team manages when answering."
For Question 5, the label options (Holding, In Waiting, Other) feel somewhat leading toward "Holding." Recommend adding an open-text follow-up: "If you selected Other, what label would feel most natural to your team?"
New Question: Post-Video Clarity Check. Add a short clip from the Community of Practice (CoP) office hours recording via Microsoft Stream, specifically the segment where paused steps and cancellation permissions were discussed live. This gives respondents who didn't attend the meeting a chance to hear the context and common questions before answering. Place this before the final section with a note like: "Didn't attend office hours? Watch this short clip from our recent CoP session where these features were discussed live."
Then consider adding the question: "After reviewing the survey content (and the CoP recording if applicable), do you still have questions about either feature?"

☐ Yes
☐ No

If Yes, optionally branch to: "What questions do you still have?" (open text). This closes the feedback loop and helps the team identify where communication gaps remain.

Section 2: Requestor Cancellation Permissions
Since this is a completely separate and unrelated feature from Section 1, make that explicit at the top of the section. Add a brief orienting note like: "This next section covers a separate LEAF feature unrelated to workflow step pausing. Here we'd like your input on requestor cancellation permissions." This prevents respondents from mentally conflating the two topics.

At the top of this section like the last section, explicitly call out the affected LEAF feature e.g.: "LEAF Core Feature: Forms" This helps users immediately anchor the context to something they know and use.

For Question 8, the yes/no framing is too blunt for a nuanced topic. Consider splitting it: first ask whether cancellation should be allowed at all, then conditionally ask at which stage, with options like: Before any approvals / After first approval / At any point / Other. This will produce far more actionable data.
For Question 11, the open-ended prompt needs scaffolding. Recommend adding guided sub-prompts beneath it: "Consider: Who should be notified when a request is cancelled? Should a reason be required? Should there be a confirmation step before cancellation is finalized?"

Section 3: Stay Connected

Enable branching in Microsoft Forms so the email field only appears when the respondent selects "Yes" to follow-up contact. Since Forms supports this natively, there's no reason to show the email field to everyone.
Enable the "Allow respondents to save their responses" setting in Microsoft Forms so respondents can save their progress and return later. This is especially helpful for thoughtful, longer responses and reduces incomplete submissions.
Add a closing thank-you message after submission: "Thank you for your time and input! Your feedback will directly shape how these features are developed. We'll share updates as decisions are made (in office hours or etc.)."

Deployment Timing
Per the morning standup discussion, if you choose to add the video, the survey should be sent out after the CoP office hour session. Consider adjusting the initial deployment date accordingly so respondents have the benefit of that live discussion before completing the survey. The CoP recording clip should be embedded in the survey itself (via Microsoft Stream URL) so that even those who couldn't attend have access to the same context.
